.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt NC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nc
.Nd TCP client
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ss os-simple
.Nm
.Ar host port
.Ss os-real
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar host port
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Fl l
.Fl p Ar port
.Op Ar host
.Op Ar port
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On os-simple,
.Nm
is the project's TCP client.
It connects to
.Ar host
at
.Ar port ,
writes one chunk of standard input to the socket, reads one reply chunk,
and writes that reply to standard output.
It repeats until end of input or an error.
Each read takes at most 1024 bytes.
Input chunks need not contain complete lines.
A reply that spans several reads is printed one read for each chunk of
input, and what the peer sends after the end of input is not read.
Both output paths complete partial writes.
A read that returns 0 or fails ends the transfer with status 0;
a write failure ends it with status 1.
.Pp
The host is an IPv4 address or a name resolved as described in
.Xr ping 1 .
The port must be decimal digits from 1 through 65535; a sign, a trailing
character, or a value past 65535 is refused.
.Pp
On os-real,
.Nm
is BusyBox's applet, with the options below.
.Sh OPTIONS
These options apply to the BusyBox applet on os-real:
.Bl -tag -width "-e program"
.It Fl b
Allow broadcasts.
.It Fl e Ar program
Run the program after connecting.
This option must be last.
.It Fl i Ar seconds
Delay this many seconds between lines sent.
.It Fl k
With
.Fl l
and
.Fl e ,
provide a persistent server.
.It Fl l
Listen for incoming connections.
.It Fl n
Suppress the reverse name lookup in verbose reports of incoming
connections; host operands are still resolved.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write a hexadecimal dump of traffic to the file.
.It Fl p Ar port
Use this local port.
.It Fl s Ar address
Use this local address.
.It Fl u
Use UDP.
.It Fl v
Produce verbose output.
.It Fl w Ar seconds
Set the timeout for connections and final network reads.
.It Fl z
Use zero-I/O mode for scanning.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl nc 10.0.2.2 80
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The project client prints
.Ql "usage: nc <host> <port>"
when a host or port operand is missing.
A port that is not decimal digits from 1 through 65535 produces
.Ql "nc: invalid port: PORT: not a number from 1 to 65535" .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr ping 1 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
